    "summary": "High‑energy Good Lovin' and Jorma’s guest jam shine through",
    "blurb": "The concert features a punchy two‑part Good Lovin' sandwich with a tight drum break in between, capped by an electrifying late‑set jam that brings guest guitarist Jorma into the mix. The three electric sets flow naturally, delivering solid early‑night material and a memorable improvisational climax.",
    "review": "On November 20, 1970 at the Palestra, the Dead rolled out a classic three‑electric‑set marathon that still feels fresh for listeners today. Set 1 opens with familiar folk‑rock staples before launching into a high‑octane Good Lovin' that bookends a brief drum interlude – a moment of pure, unfiltered energy captured even on an audience cassette. The second half of the set pushes deeper into improvisation, highlighted by a seamless transition from The Other One into Saint Stephen and a rolling Not Fade Away medley that keeps the momentum alive. Set 2 is a jam‑heavy affair, culminating in Uncle John’s Band after a series of loose jams. The real show‑stopper arrives late in the night when Jorma Kaukonen drops in for an exuberant guest jam that lifts the crowd and adds a unique flavor to an otherwise solid ’70 performance. Despite cassette‑generation dropouts, the tape retains enough clarity to let the band’s chemistry and the audience’s excitement shine through.",
